5. Transaction Handling for Complex Operations
Creating an Invoice with Items
* Wrap creation in a single transaction to ensure atomicity
* Implementation: BEGIN;-- Insert invoiceINSERT INTO invoices (...) VALUES (...) RETURNING id;-- Insert invoice items using the returned invoice idINSERT INTO invoice_items (...) VALUES (...);INSERT INTO invoice_items (...) VALUES (...);-- Commit if all operations succeedCOMMIT;
* 
Updating a Client Affecting Multiple Invoices
* For Supabase implementation, use client-side transactions: const { data, error } = await supabase.rpc('update_client_with_invoices', {  p_client_id: clientId,  p_updated_client: updatedClientObject});
* 
Batch Updates for Client Statistics
* Create a stored procedure for efficient batch updates: CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_client_statistics(  client_ids UUID[]) RETURNS VOID AS $$BEGIN  UPDATE clients  SET    amount = subquery.total_amount,    outstanding_amount = subquery.outstanding,    due_amount = subquery.due,    invoice_count = subquery.count  FROM (    SELECT       client_id,      SUM(total) as total_amount,      SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'outstanding' THEN total ELSE 0 END) as outstanding,      SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'overdue' THEN total ELSE 0 END) as due,      COUNT(*) as count    FROM invoices    WHERE client_id = ANY(client_ids)    GROUP BY client_id  ) AS subquery  WHERE clients.id = subquery.client_id;END;$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
* 
